\chapter{Fixed arrays, slices, and checked offsets}
\label{ch:22}
\index{Fixed arrays, slices, and checked offsets}

A fixed array carries its element type and extent in the checked layout. Indexing tests that an index belongs to the relevant range. A slice describes a bounded segment and has its own constraints on element representation, bounds, and construction. Read the constructor and indexing rules for the intended backend, since standard MIR and the VM product profiles admit different layouts.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn main()
{
  let values = array[3, 5, 8];
  assert(values[1] == 5);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

A boundary test should read the first and last valid elements, then exercise an invalid one-past-end access independently. Also test slicing at the boundary, where an empty slice may be well formed and the same position is still invalid for indexing. MEMGraph can prove specific fixed-array accesses nontrapping at compile time and elide their checks. It leaves dynamic or unproved cases intact. \cref{ch:77} discusses how to inspect that decision.

\section{Translate two sets of coordinates}
Use the fixture's four samples: 10, 20, 30 and 40. The slice from
position one up to position three has length two; its elements at local
indices zero and one are 20 and 30. This explains why the accumulation
over its own length gives 50 rather than 90. Take a second slice of that
slice, from one up to two. Its local element zero is now the original
element two, namely 30. Testing the second range against the capacity
four alone would be an error: first check the requested endpoints
against the current logical length, then compose the offset with the
current start.

In this profile the slice carries a snapshot of the backing array.
Imagine binding the original samples to a mutable variable, making the
first slice, and replacing the whole array with four zeros. The sum of
the saved slice is still 50. This experiment distinguishes a snapshot
from a view into the variable even though reading both before replacement
would give identical numbers. Length and capacity answer different
questions, too: the snapshot has room for the backing value but does not
authorize a caller to read outside the saved segment. Returning the slice
from the producing function therefore needs neither an extended loan of
the local array nor permission to index all four positions.

